  "slug": "imaging-photosynthetic-living-plants-1563",
  "summary": "Four late-November 2026 preprints dissect proton and pH dynamics controlling photosynthetic light regulation in Arabidopsis and Chlamydomonas.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the control of proton and ion fluxes across the thylakoid membrane and their coupling to photosynthetic regulation. A mutagenesis study in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii identifies conserved subunit-IV residue PetD:D35 as essential for proton resupply and Q-cycle efficiency at the cytochrome b6f stromal Qn site. In Arabidopsis, PsbS mutants with altered lumenal protonation sites show a small, zeaxanthin- and acidification-linked energy-dependent quenching, pointing to complexity in PsbS protonation. NHD1, a thylakoid Na+/H+ antiporter, supports acclimation to fluctuating light and shows partial redundancy with KEA3 and VCCN1. A comparison of four genetically encoded pH biosensors identifies pHluorin2 for imaging light-driven stromal and lumen pH dynamics in living Arabidopsis and Chlamydomonas. What is new is the in vivo readout of these proton-handling processes alongside molecular dissection of their components.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
